    "title": "April Fools' Day 2026: History, origins and prank traditions",
    "text": "April Fools' Day is Wednesday, April 1, 2026.Nambitomo/Getty ImagesIt’s time for jokesters and pranksters to try out their best tricks — Wednesday is April Fools' Day.Humorous pranks and tricks have been entertaining the world for centuries. There’s nothing quite like plotting a harmless joke on loved ones — and the more creative, the better.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢More than the average day, April Fools' Day is for the pranksters and jokesters. If you needed an excuse to initiate some lighthearted fun, this is it. But how did the holiday come into existence? It’s much older than you probably think.Here’s a history lesson on the holiday of hoaxes.When is April Fools' Day 2026?April Fools' Day is always celebrated on April 1, which falls on a Wednesday this year.Article continues below this adWhat is April Fools' Day? Many historians trace April Fools' Day back to 1582, though the History Channel and the Library of Congress report its exact origins are unclear.That year, France changed its calendar system from the Julian calendar to the Gregorian calendar. The Gregorian calendar, which is the system we now use, shows the new year as starting on January 1. However, the Julian calendar showed the new year began on the spring equinox, which usually fell around April 1.Article continues below this adMany in France were unaware of the calendar change and continued to celebrate the new year during the last week of March through April 1. These people were considered the “butt of jokes and hoaxes” and were called “April fools,” according to the History Channel.Others believe that April Fools' derives from the Roman festival of Hilaria, which is Latin for “joyful.” Hilaria was celebrated at the end of March by devotees of the goddess Cybele, the History Channel reports. During the festival, followers would dress up in disguises and trick townspeople.The History Channel describes yet another group who speculates April Fools' was related to the first day of spring, \"when Mother Nature fooled people with changing, unpredictable weather.\"Why is it called April Fools' Day?Jesters of the 14th century. Fool in yellow tunic blowing a dog like bagpipes from Guyart des Moulins' Bible historiale, f.262, Royal MS 15 D iii, jester in motley or parti-coloured costume with bells holding an owl and stick from Breviary of John the Fearless and Margaret of Bavaria, Harley MS 2897, King David's fool with marotte from the Princess Joan Psalter, Royal MS 2 B viii, f.54, and fool with pig's bladder on a stick from Harley MS 2840. Handcoloured engraving by Joseph Strutt from his Complete View of the Dress and Habits of the People of England, Henry Bohn, London, 1842.Florilegius/Universal Images Group via Getty ImagesWhile it is believed April Fools' became more established in the 1580s, the idea of pranking someone on this day was first referenced a few decades prior, according to the Library of Congress. A 1561 Flemish poem by Eduard De Dene chronicles a servant who was sent on a series of pointless errands. When the servant noticed that the date was April 1, he realized he had been sent on “fool’s errands.”Article continues below this adIn 1686, John Aubrey mentions “Fooles Holy Day” in the book “Remaines of Gentilisme and Judaisme,” writing “We observe it on the first of April.”The “April fish” is one of many April Fools' pranks. It comes from the French “poisson d’avril.” Paper fish are often put on someone’s back as a joke because the fish are said to be young and, therefore, easily caught. Those who get the fish placed on their backs are seen as gullible.Article continues below this adIn Scotland, April Fools' Day spans two days. The first day is “Gowk Day,” referencing the gawk, or cuckoo bird, which is a symbol of the fool. The second day is “Tailie Day” when people will play pranks, such as pinning a tail on someone or putting a “kick me” sign on their back, the History Channel reports.— Olivia Munson, USA TODAY Network, contributed to this report.Get alerted to the most important stories and stay on top of the news.April 1, 2026Alexis Simmerman has been a trending reporter for the Austin American-Statesman since January 2024. She is a 2023 graduate of the Missouri School of Journalism and has a Bachelor of Arts in English from the University of Missouri.",
